schema: "0.4"
id: fim-notebook-livebook-and-widgets-kino-process-02-dense-reactive-stress-widget
type: visualization
quality: high
solution: kino-process
category: notebook-livebook-and-widgets
variant: dense-reactive-stress-widget
prompt:
  system: |-
    You are a senior Elixir Livebook runtime engineer. Output only one complete ASCII Elixir Livebook code cell for Kino.Process. No Markdown fences, no prose outside code. Prioritize scoped process visualization and safe runtime inspection.
  text: |-
    Create a dense reactive Kino.Process stress cell. Start or discover a bounded local supervision tree with multiple worker processes, render a scoped Kino.Process.sup_tree/2 rather than an unreadable whole-node tree, and provide a metrics table built from Process.info/2 for top local processes by memory or mailbox length. Include an optional safe Kino.animate/3 wrapper to refresh the scoped tree at a modest interval.

    Requirements: document Livebook compatibility, snapshot versus refresh behavior, process metric data shape, and tracing overhead; avoid app_tree/2 on unknown huge applications; do not call Kino.Process.info/1 or memory_usage/0; include accessibility summary text and fallback local tree creation; include safe execution checks for production nodes, busy systems, credentials, eval, shell calls, and unbounded Process.list scanning.
  provider_options:
    max_tokens: 8192
    temperature: 0.2
    top_p: 0.9
output:
  text_format: elixir
  artifact_kind: livebook-kino-process-stress-cell
  formats:
  - format: exs
evaluation:
  checklist:
  - "Import check: generated code installs kino, renders Kino.Process.sup_tree/2 or app_tree/2, and uses Process.info/2 for metrics."
  - "Notebook compatibility: comments identify Livebook/Kino and explain snapshot versus bounded refresh."
  - "Reactive state: optional Kino.animate refresh is scoped, modest, and documented."
  - "Data shape: process pid/name/status/memory/mailbox fields and supervisor child data are documented."
  - "Accessibility and fallback: process tree summary, metrics table, and fallback local tree are readable."
  - "Safe execution: no fake Kino.Process info APIs, production-wide tracing, unbounded scans, credentials, eval, shell commands, or remote node assumptions."
  rubric:
  - name: Format validity
    weight: 30
    criteria:
    - "Output is one complete ASCII Elixir Livebook code cell with no Markdown fences or explanatory wrapper."
    - "The code is valid and uses scoped current Kino.Process APIs."
    - "Metrics use Process.info/2 and supported table rendering."
  - name: Semantic completeness
    weight: 30
    criteria:
    - "Implements a dense process inspection workflow with scoped tree visualization, metrics, and optional refresh."
    - "Accurately distinguishes Kino.Process diagram APIs from standard process metrics APIs."
    - "Includes notebook/livebook compatibility, reactive state, data shape, accessibility, fallback, and safe execution checks."
  - name: UX/structural quality
    weight: 25
    criteria:
    - "The tree is scoped for legibility and paired with a useful metrics table."
    - "Refresh status and performance notes are easy to understand."
    - "Text summaries make diagram content accessible."
  - name: Robustness
    weight: 15
    criteria:
    - "Handles dead pids, empty process lists, missing app trees, large trees, and high mailbox values."
    - "Uses bounded process sampling and modest refresh intervals."
    - "Documents tracing and polling risks."
  pass_conditions:
  - "All required YAML fields are present and the generated artifact is one valid ASCII Elixir Livebook cell."
  - "Rubric weights are exactly Format validity 30, Semantic completeness 30, UX/structural quality 25, Robustness 15."
  - "The prompt requires notebook/livebook compatibility, reactive state, data shape, accessibility, fallback, and safe execution checks."
  - "The solution remains Kino.Process-centered and avoids unbounded runtime inspection."
tags:
- fim
- notebook-livebook-and-widgets
- livebook
- kino-process
- dense-reactive-stress-widget

